\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) generalize well semantically but, being pre-trained mainly on high-level understanding tasks such as VQA, under-emphasize low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics needed for precise manipulation. Prediction-oriented pre-training has the opposite profile. We present UP-VLA, a unified VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) and future visual prediction (PRE) in a single autoregressive backbone, and generates actions conditioned on both a scene description and predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average length of 4.08 versus 3.06 for GR-1 (a 33\% improvement), and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D versus 4.21. On a Franka Panda, it improves unseen-object grasping and precise operations. Ablations show prediction is crucial for spatial grounding and MMU for semantic generalization.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large VLMs have been used to initialize robot policies for better open-world generalization \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla,black2024pi_0}. Yet VLMs are predominantly pre-trained on high-level tasks like VQA \citep{liu2024visual}, so they tend to neglect low-level visual and spatial cues (depth, size, relative pose) and physical dynamics, which matter for precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes. Conversely, prediction-based policies that pre-train on future frame or video generation \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero,du2024learning} acquire spatial grounding and physical consistency but lack semantic robustness. This dichotomy (\cref{fig:intro}) motivates a model that holds both abilities.

