\Cref{fig:cmp} contrasts the three families. VLM-based VLAs inherit open-world semantics from web-scale image-text pre-training, but that pre-training emphasizes what is in an image rather than where things are precisely or how scenes evolve; as a result they struggle with fine-grained control. Prediction-based models learn from robot video and so capture spatial layout and physical consistency, yet they lack the language-grounded semantic robustness of VLMs and generalize less well to new objects. UP-VLA is designed so that a single model receives supervision of both kinds: understanding from large-scale image-text data, and dynamics from robot video and action data. The rest of the paper studies whether this combination yields gains in simulation and on a real robot, and which of its components are responsible.

\section{Related Works}
\textbf{VLM-based VLAs.} RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt} and OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla} fine-tune VLMs to output actions, inheriting semantic generalization; RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision} adapts a VLM to language-conditioned manipulation, and $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0} builds a generalist flow-based policy on a VLM. 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d} adds 3D inputs and a generative world model. These models rely on understanding-oriented pre-training and thus see little low-level dynamics supervision.

\textbf{Prediction-based policies.} GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} pre-trains a GPT-style model on video prediction; SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} and UniPi \citep{du2024learning} generate future images or videos as subgoals; related ideas include video language planning \citep{du2023video} and joint denoising of prediction and action \citep{guo2024prediction}. These improve spatial and physical grounding but lack the semantic knowledge of VLMs.

\textbf{Unified understanding and generation.} Models such as Show-o \citep{xie2024show} and DreamLLM \citep{dong2023dreamllm} combine understanding and generation in one model. UP-VLA brings this idea to embodied control, and uses a VQ-GAN tokenizer \citep{esser2021taming} for prediction alongside a CLIP encoder \citep{radford2021learning} for understanding.

\textbf{Positioning.} Compared with RT-2-style VLAs, which fine-tune a VLM directly to emit actions, UP-VLA adds an explicit future-prediction objective during pre-training and action learning, so action tokens can attend to predicted visual tokens. Compared with GR-1-style prediction models, UP-VLA keeps the multi-modal understanding objective on image-text data and uses its output, a scene description, as an additional conditioning input for actions. We include both reductions as controlled baselines built on the same Phi-1.5 backbone (UP-VLA-RT-2 and UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu), which isolates the effect of the training paradigm from backbone choice.

\section{Preliminaries}
We consider language-conditioned manipulation: given an instruction $L$ and observation $O_t$, a policy predicts a chunk of actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. We use two image pathways. A continuous encoder (CLIP-ViT with MLP projection $E_1$) maps an image into the language embedding space as tokens $u$. A discrete VQ-GAN tokenizer $E_2$ maps images to codebook indices $v$; a VQ decoder reconstructs images from predicted indices. All encoders are frozen; the language backbone, Phi-1.5 \citep{li2023textbooks}, is fully fine-tuned.

\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Unified prompting and attention masks}
UP-VLA is an autoregressive model with one LLM backbone that performs (i) multi-modal understanding, (ii) future image token prediction and (iii) action generation (\cref{fig:overview}); task-specific prompts and attention masks (\cref{fig:mask}) select the behavior.

\textbf{MMU.} The input is \{[MMU], image tokens $u$, language tokens $l$\}. Image tokens precede text so they can attend to each other, and the LLM predicts next language tokens: $\hat{H}_L=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$.

\textbf{PRE.} The input is \{[PRE], language tokens $l$, current image tokens $v_t$\}. Image tokens follow the text so they attend to all prior tokens; future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions rather than as next-token prediction over the sequence: $\hat{H}_{O_{t+\Delta t}}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$.

