\textbf{Design rationale for the two token streams.} Understanding benefits from continuous CLIP features that carry rich semantics, whereas autoregressive image prediction is most naturally cast as classification over a discrete codebook, so we use both pathways rather than forcing a single representation. The mask design follows from the tasks: placing image tokens before text in MMU lets the image tokens attend to each other as in standard VLM practice, while placing them after text in PRE lets every image position attend to the full instruction and to the preceding image tokens, and predictions are made at aligned positions so that the sequence produces the whole future frame rather than a continuation of the current one. Because the encoders are frozen, only the LLM and the lightweight policy head are trained, and the discrete tokenizer provides a reconstruction path (VQ decoder) for visualizing predictions.

\subsection{Joint prediction and understanding for action}
Given $(O_t, L)$, we first obtain a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ (e.g. ``describe this image''), embed additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$, and form an extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. The model then jointly predicts future image tokens and actions, $(\hat{H}_{O_{t+\Delta t}},\hat{H}_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L')$. A lightweight policy head (single-layer attention pooling, MAP, plus an MLP) maps action tokens to continuous actions: $\hat{a}_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat{H}_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}))$. Actions thus attend to both semantic (MMU) and predictive visual tokens. At test time we repeat this procedure autoregressively, executing the decoded actions.

\textbf{Test-time procedure.} At each step the agent (1) receives the current image $O_t$ and instruction $L$; (2) generates a scene description $s$ with the MMU pathway and embeds additional visual features $E_1(O'_t)$; (3) forms $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$ and runs the PRE pathway to obtain predicted future image tokens and action tokens; (4) decodes actions with MAP and the MLP and executes them; and (5) repeats. The predicted image tokens are produced together with the actions and serve as an intermediate representation that the action tokens attend to.

\subsection{Training}
The MMU loss is next-token cross-entropy, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}=-\sum_i \log p_\theta(l_i\mid u,l_{1:i-1})$. The prediction loss is cross-entropy over future image tokens, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}=-\sum_{j=1}^{M}\log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$. The action loss is $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\|\hat a_{\mathrm{pos}}-a_{\mathrm{pos}}\|_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat a_{\mathrm{end}},a_{\mathrm{end}})$. The total is $\mathcal{L}=\lambda_1\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}$.

Training has two stages. \emph{Pre-training} mixes MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and prediction data (25k Bridge demonstrations, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}) with task-specific prompts and masks. \emph{Action fine-tuning} uses downstream robot data with the joint prompt, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic capability.

\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Setup}
\textbf{Simulation.} We use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D (train on scenes A, B, C; test on unseen D) and ABCD$\rightarrow$D settings, reporting success rates of 1--5 chained tasks and average completed length. \textbf{Real world.} A Franka Emika Panda with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills; we evaluate seen tasks, unseen-object grasping and precise operations (e.g. cable routing, small-object grasping), 20 trials per task (\cref{fig:env}). \textbf{Baselines.} VLA-based: RoboFlamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2 (RT-2-style action fine-tuning of our backbone), RT-1; prediction-based: GR-1, SuSIE, Uni-Pi, 3D Diffuser Actor, Diffusion Policy; and UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu, a GR-1-like model with the same backbone.

