\textbf{Metrics and protocol.} In CALVIN the agent must complete five instructions chained in sequence, zero-shot on a held-out instruction chain set. We report the fraction of rollouts completing at least $k$ tasks in a row ($k=1,\dots,5$) and the average length, i.e. the expected number of consecutively completed tasks. ABC$\rightarrow$D tests generalization to a novel environment D after training on A, B and C, whereas ABCD$\rightarrow$D tests in-distribution scenes. Real-world success is a fraction over 20 trials per task.

