On ABC$\rightarrow$D (\cref{table1}), UP-VLA attains an average length of 4.08, versus 3.06 for GR-1 (+33\%), 3.35 for 3D Diffuser Actor, 2.47 for RoboFlamingo and 1.44 for UP-VLA-RT-2. Baselines are copied from original papers except starred rows, which we reproduced. The gap is largest at longer horizons (69.9\% five-task success versus 40.1\% for GR-1). On ABCD$\rightarrow$D (\cref{table2}), UP-VLA reaches 4.42 versus 4.21 (GR-1) and 4.09 (RoboFlamingo). Results are reported as single values; we have no variance estimates across seeds.

\input{tables/table_jj.tex}

The comparison with the baselines separates the sources of improvement. UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu, which shares our backbone but drops understanding, scores 3.13, close to GR-1 (3.06), while the RT-2-style variant with the same backbone scores only 1.44. So the backbone alone does not explain the 4.08 of UP-VLA: it is the combination of prediction and understanding that lifts performance by roughly 1.0 average length over either single-objective variant. Success on the first task is similar to 3D Diffuser Actor (0.928 vs. 0.938) but the gap widens with horizon (0.699 vs. 0.412 at five tasks), consistent with stronger error resilience over long chains. On ABCD$\rightarrow$D, where all methods are strong, UP-VLA still leads in tasks 2 to 5 and in average length (4.42), although the margin over GR-1 (0.21) is smaller than in the harder ABC$\rightarrow$D setting (1.02), indicating the largest benefit arises under distribution shift. The one metric where UP-VLA does not lead on ABCD$\rightarrow$D is first-task success (0.962, versus 0.964 for RoboFlamingo).

