\subsection{Discussion}
Taken together, the ablations and baselines indicate complementary roles. Prediction supplies low-level spatial and dynamics information: removing it, or removing predictive pre-training on Bridge, causes the largest drops on CALVIN, and the precision-task ordering favors the prediction-based variant over the RT-2-style one. Understanding supplies semantic generalization: removing it leaves seen-task performance essentially unchanged but cuts unseen-object success from 0.58 to 0.20, and the RT-2-style variant, which retains a VLM's semantics, beats the prediction-only variant on unseen-object grasping. The MMU-conditioned action mechanism adds a further gain (unseen 0.50 to 0.58, CALVIN 3.99 to 4.08). These conclusions rest on single runs and 20-trial real-world evaluations, so the smaller differences should be read as suggestive rather than conclusive.

\section{Limitations}
Prediction shows artifacts such as background color shifts across scenes, indicating insufficient visual generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU occasionally misclassifies objects, reflecting backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and curated data could reduce this. Joint tasks add training cost and sequence length; parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory may help. Extending to 3D inputs, multiple views or explicit physics may further improve spatial reasoning.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures. It improves CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D from 3.06 to 4.08 average length over GR-1 and transfers to real-world semantic and precision tasks, offering a path toward generalist robot policies with both high-level and low-level competence.

